{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 622 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a0b6325de9e9f1983cd3e6bebb97034f0837bbe6ef267c614c6dd68c69e2fdd9" :edb/db-before-t 621 :edb/db-after-t 622 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533934 50 #inst "2026-10-05T14:31:43.060Z" 13194139533934 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266981 13194139533934 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266984 13194139533934 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266989 13194139533934 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186267015 13194139533934 true] [17592186096627 1020 #edb/ref 17592186267029 13194139533934 true] [17592186096628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266953 13194139533934 true] [17592186096628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266960 13194139533934 true] [17592186096628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186267001 13194139533934 true] [17592186096628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186267008 13194139533934 true] [17592186096628 1020 #edb/ref 17592186267022 13194139533934 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266967 13194139533934 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266974 13194139533934 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266995 13194139533934 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186266998 13194139533934 true] [17592186096629 1020 #edb/ref 17592186267036 13194139533934 true] [17592186266953 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266954 13194139533934 true] [17592186266953 1089 "q-14185" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266953 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533934 true] [17592186266953 1100 "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}-x+10y+21=0$ with the $x$-axis." 13194139533934 true] [17592186266953 1113 #uuid "4afbc9de-3c3c-55ec-9e08-58a688816d26" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266953 1145 "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x+10y+21 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}-x+10(0)+21 & =0 \\\\ -x+21 & =0 \\\\ x & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(21,0).$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266955 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266956 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266957 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266958 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266959 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1041 "selection" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266958 13194139533934 true] [17592186266954 1110 #uuid "3bfa9bf3-3154-5936-b799-ba6ffe85b307" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266955 1034 "(-21,0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266955 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266955 1111 #uuid "9a5b2f28-7e99-5ecf-a292-17f279a75ec1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266956 1034 "(-12,0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266956 1111 #uuid "613a366c-b86d-555b-aea8-9c2f39fcb1fb" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266957 1034 "(0,21)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266957 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266957 1111 #uuid "e78bcb29-5e58-5fc7-a969-cc8d4ffe0125" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266958 1034 "(21,0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266958 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266958 1111 #uuid "eb6c8391-2974-504a-aa15-751b8cacbd8c" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266959 1034 "(12,0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266959 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266959 1111 #uuid "f5aa179e-eb82-5213-8c4d-ee6e8aa1106b" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266960 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266961 13194139533934 true] [17592186266960 1089 "q-14190" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266960 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533934 true] [17592186266960 1100 "Find the point of intersection of the parabola ${y}^{2}+18x-27y+54=0$ with the $x$-axis." 13194139533934 true] [17592186266960 1113 #uuid "0d20df8a-265e-56c9-b923-e820fa174518" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266960 1145 "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}+18x-27(0)+54 & =0 \\\\ 18x+54 & =0 \\\\ 18x & =-54 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{54}{18} \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at $(-3,0).$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266962 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266963 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266964 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266965 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266966 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1041 "selection" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266966 13194139533934 true] [17592186266961 1110 #uuid "ba701a28-f86f-5150-94b9-bd55824337d9" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266962 1034 "(-54,18)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266962 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266962 1111 #uuid "a18217e9-d001-5644-9eda-1138e048a596" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266963 1034 "(3,0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266963 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266963 1111 #uuid "eb23b95d-d6e7-5692-8678-aa322bf0f8c7" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266964 1034 "(\\frac{1}{3},0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266964 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266964 1111 #uuid "28eccccf-0e85-511d-bb72-ec24635326d3" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266965 1034 "(-\\frac{1}{3},0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266965 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266965 1111 #uuid "13d4b897-395d-57e1-acc2-0b21f187590f" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266966 1034 "(-3,0)" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266966 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266966 1111 #uuid "1542c926-cc2a-51ca-80df-91da3851fc60" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266967 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266968 13194139533934 true] [17592186266967 1089 "q-165433" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266967 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533934 true] [17592186266967 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/616116b9e5cf137c526b11dc5ce1647cd8f9cbd3a652be6dade9a8769467c27c.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266967 1113 #uuid "d8bf318e-dc63-500a-966e-f40512cc20a3" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266967 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(5,15)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(5,15)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}=4p(y-15).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ we substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-5)^{2} & =4p(6-15) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5)^{2} & =-(y-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-5)^{2} & =-(y-15) \\\\ 25 & =-y+15 \\\\ y & =15-25 \\\\ y & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-10.$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266969 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266970 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266971 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266972 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266973 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1041 "selection" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266970 13194139533934 true] [17592186266968 1110 #uuid "1e088981-d698-536c-8dbb-b27115580bc5" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266969 1034 "-9" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266969 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266969 1111 #uuid "ab0feba6-4ed8-5c50-aba8-7a6a1ba9b689" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266970 1034 "-10" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266970 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266970 1111 #uuid "7ce16ccf-bf1a-5e0f-95f0-a49070129e73" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266971 1034 "-\\sqrt{15}" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266971 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266971 1111 #uuid "23c483bf-8201-5984-8008-743dbbcb908d" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266972 1034 "-\\sqrt{20}" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266972 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266972 1111 #uuid "fafa2fe9-c298-54ae-9840-3a425a91e6d2" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266973 1034 "-12" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266973 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266973 1111 #uuid "bebf9dd7-d488-5833-8a26-6291e0a0e012" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266974 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266975 13194139533934 true] [17592186266974 1089 "q-165434" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266974 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533934 true] [17592186266974 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/6450376de8f9f4c0c8f5b414d95de67839664eda4f2056376160caddef47ba1f.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266974 1113 #uuid "20250ecc-bc9a-594d-a7f9-8fe791acfa2b" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266974 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(\\frac{9}{2},1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4p(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(4,0),$ we substitute $x=4$ and $y=0$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =4p(4-\\frac{9}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}=-2(x-\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2} & =(0+9) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $-2$ and $4.$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266976 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266977 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266978 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266979 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266980 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1041 "selection" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266978 13194139533934 true] [17592186266975 1110 #uuid "86262259-005d-57ec-85bf-24880b46069d" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266976 1034 "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $3$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266976 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533934 true] [17592186266976 1111 #uuid "eac601b7-b428-57ab-a182-f986c1ddc836" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266977 1034 "$-1$ and $3$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266977 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533934 true] [17592186266977 1111 #uuid "39b2902f-7269-55ed-b64d-21a49e01c410" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266978 1034 "$-2$ and $4$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266978 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533934 true] [17592186266978 1111 #uuid "5e8b9bd4-fac1-5905-a346-2c5785dccf75" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266979 1034 "$-1$ and $4$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266979 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533934 true] [17592186266979 1111 #uuid "0d286ef6-36b7-521a-8fe1-d87f2694928a" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266980 1034 "$-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $2$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266980 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139533934 true] [17592186266980 1111 #uuid "c61de2ee-64f4-588a-b4d3-0aebd6c40656" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266981 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266982 13194139533934 true] [17592186266981 1089 "q-240666" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266981 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533934 true] [17592186266981 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/d4c21d6ce09ff32004a28d18d223fef7858c377bccb0eb532cf2dda29a545f5e.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." 13194139533934 true] [17592186266981 1113 #uuid "295ec8a5-8065-5b04-8ad1-001208416bc9" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266981 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-7,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-11,-6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-7,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+2)^{2}=4p(x+7).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-11,-6),$ we substitute $x=-11$ and $y=-6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6+2)^{2} & =4p(-11+7) \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 16 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =-4(x+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =-4(x+7) \\\\ 4 & =-4(x+7) \\\\ -1 & =x+7 \\\\ x & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-8.$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266982 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266983 13194139533934 true] [17592186266982 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533934 true] [17592186266982 1041 "field-1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266982 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266983 13194139533934 true] [17592186266982 1110 #uuid "dd19f6a9-87d8-507c-90b8-34b2f77c5a4a" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266983 1034 "-8" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266983 1111 #uuid "27e7a0d0-07af-5e4e-b493-9c279e82c57b" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266984 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266985 13194139533934 true] [17592186266984 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266987 13194139533934 true] [17592186266984 1089 "q-251699" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266984 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533934 true] [17592186266984 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/0da514f806c56689fdf4be724223e75c42559aa16ae38470bf088a83d299aaa9.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the values of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533934 true] [17592186266984 1113 #uuid "d5d338c0-23c4-51e8-bf62-58aee9a4f5b8" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266984 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-8)$ and passes through the point $(5,10).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-8)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,10),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=10$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+1)^{2} & =4p(10+8) \\\\ 36 & =4p(18) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =2(y+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm 4-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-5$  and $3.$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266985 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266986 13194139533934 true] [17592186266985 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533934 true] [17592186266985 1041 "field-1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266985 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266986 13194139533934 true] [17592186266985 1110 #uuid "f0ed16da-cc28-5863-9b17-bcd8a2c0b6df" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266986 1034 "-5" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266986 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266986 1111 #uuid "8e655a02-fa82-562a-8cb5-966692875652" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266987 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266988 13194139533934 true] [17592186266987 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533934 true] [17592186266987 1041 "field-2" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266987 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266988 13194139533934 true] [17592186266987 1110 #uuid "ef1585fd-d627-5dcf-91f7-8cbfb940c537" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266988 1034 "3" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266988 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266988 1111 #uuid "eb8eca46-9fc7-5f1e-89e5-fa5942c1ab85" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266989 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266990 13194139533934 true] [17592186266989 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266993 13194139533934 true] [17592186266989 1089 "q-251784" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266989 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533934 true] [17592186266989 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/afb7abacdffafd634165fd1f35868878b2bc420df6a173b48f6a17c7638f7dec.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the **exact values** of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533934 true] [17592186266989 1113 #uuid "1cc0f48b-955c-5fb4-87fd-77527333645f" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266989 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-5).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-3,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =4p(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-5),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-5$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =4p(-5-4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(-9) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =-(0-4) \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x+3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-5$  and $-1.$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266991 13194139533934 true] [17592186266990 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266992 13194139533934 true] [17592186266990 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533934 true] [17592186266990 1041 "field-1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266990 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266991 13194139533934 true] [17592186266990 1110 #uuid "a9c809e1-762d-5da3-930e-0f0f033735d7" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266991 1034 "-5" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266991 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266991 1111 #uuid "946e9495-5411-5a32-9d15-55fc79e6d96c" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266992 1034 "5" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266992 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266992 1111 #uuid "0c88c2c7-dac5-5150-a9a6-58bac57745c4" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266993 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266994 13194139533934 true] [17592186266993 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533934 true] [17592186266993 1041 "field-2" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266993 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266994 13194139533934 true] [17592186266993 1110 #uuid "c38e5073-3a93-5058-ae96-d40f2f5e2a0a" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266994 1034 "-1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266994 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266994 1111 #uuid "2a7abc9f-2db1-5402-8d85-39f7687436e4" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266995 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266996 13194139533934 true] [17592186266995 1089 "q-251799" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266995 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533934 true] [17592186266995 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/d589816d2368a450c0b2bbc1d3c6323b09b9d6dc5272f67a505b80f0688b16ae.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." 13194139533934 true] [17592186266995 1113 #uuid "4337d6a2-0dad-5b6f-9de4-86a4ea9d212e" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266995 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-3,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-7,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-3,-2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}=4p(y+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,6),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7+3)^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2} & =2(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =2(y+2) \\\\ 9 & =2(y+2) \\\\ \\frac{9}{2} & =y+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{2}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $\\frac{5}{2}.$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266996 1000 #edb/ref 17592186266997 13194139533934 true] [17592186266996 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533934 true] [17592186266996 1041 "field-1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266996 1104 #edb/ref 17592186266997 13194139533934 true] [17592186266996 1110 #uuid "35ce529b-8c25-5991-9fc9-9bc09b935ddd" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266997 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266997 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186266997 1111 #uuid "b0a5ef85-a6ba-5f22-bbd7-e710a552ca28" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266998 1017 #edb/ref 17592186266999 13194139533934 true] [17592186266998 1089 "q-251810" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266998 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533934 true] [17592186266998 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13955472/assets/31618ee78ebe4834cf76aedebce7c25eca8413c6a4b9d91761ccc38dbd32b01d.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." 13194139533934 true] [17592186266998 1113 #uuid "f600f675-ff18-55ea-865f-e9845a197e99" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266998 1145 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,2)$ and passes through the point $(-2,-2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,-2),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=-2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(-2-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-4) \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =-4(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =-4(y-2) \\\\ 4 & =-4(y-2) \\\\ -1 & =y-2 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266999 1000 #edb/ref 17592186267000 13194139533934 true] [17592186266999 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533934 true] [17592186266999 1041 "field-1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186266999 1104 #edb/ref 17592186267000 13194139533934 true] [17592186266999 1110 #uuid "342eabd8-334e-540b-a0f0-fb6d6f7a0ccb" 13194139533934 true] [17592186267000 1034 "1" 13194139533934 true] [17592186267000 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533934 true] [17592186267000 1111 #uuid "57c30cdc-0333-55e2-a937-59c5db3cd652" 13194139533934 true] [17592186267001 1017 #edb/ref 17592186267002 13194139533934 true] [17592186267001 1089 "q-73766" 13194139533934 true] [17592186267001 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533934 true] [17592186267001 1100 "Find the points of intersection of the parabola ${x}^{2}=\\frac{3}{7}(y+4)$ with the $x$-axis." 13194139533934 true] [17592186267001 1113 #uuid "256517c0-a227-5f0e-b206-a7c60c3cd671" 13194139533934 true] [17592186267001 1145 "When the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{7}(0+4) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{12}{7} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{12}{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the inte